{"builder":"Make one pull request that meets every line of issue #23's acceptance list:\n- math.js exports clamp(x, lo, hi), which returns x limited to the range lo to hi\n- clamp(x, lo, hi) throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi\n- README.md documents every function math.js exports\n- npm test passes\n\nCurrent state: math.js is CommonJS and exports { add, subtract, multiply }. README.md has an \"## API\" section with \"### add(a, b)\" and \"### multiply(a, b)\" entries; subtract is exported but NOT documented. Tests are in test/math.test.js and run with `npm test` (node --test).\n\nSteps:\n1. In math.js, add a function clamp(x, lo, hi) with a short doc comment. First, if lo > hi, throw a RangeError with a clear message (e.g. `throw new RangeError('clamp: lo must not be greater than hi')`). Otherwise return x when lo <= x <= hi, lo when x < lo, and hi when x > hi (e.g. Math.min(Math.max(x, lo), hi)). Add clamp to module.exports, keeping add, subtract and multiply exported.\n2. In test/math.test.js, import clamp and add tests: value inside range returned unchanged (clamp(50, 0, 100) === 50); below range returns lo (clamp(-5, 0, 100) === 0); above returns hi (clamp(140, 0, 100) === 100); boundaries (clamp(0,0,100) === 0, clamp(100,0,100) === 100); lo === hi returns that value; and assert.throws(() => clamp(5, 10, 0), RangeError).\n3. In README.md's \"## API\" section, add a \"### subtract(a, b)\" entry (it is currently missing) and a \"### clamp(x, lo, hi)\" entry, each with a one-line description and a small code example, matching the style of the existing entries. The clamp entry must say it limits x to the range lo..hi and throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi. Make sure every name in math.js's module.exports has a \"### name(\" heading.\n4. Run `npm test` and confirm it exits 0 with no failing tests.\n5. Open the pull request referencing issue #23.","verifier":"Check out the PR head and run each check below from the repository root, in order. Report PASS or FAIL for each with the command output that decided it.\n\nCheck 1 — expect: \"math.js exports clamp(x, lo, hi), which returns x limited to the range lo to hi\"\nRun: node -e \"const m=require('./math.js'); if(typeof m.clamp!=='function'){console.log('NO CLAMP');process.exit(1)} const c=m.clamp; const cases=[[50,0,100,50],[-5,0,100,0],[140,0,100,100],[0,0,100,0],[100,0,100,100],[7,3,3,3],[-10,-20,-5,-10],[-30,-20,-5,-20],[0.5,0,1,0.5],[2.5,0,1,1]]; let bad=0; for(const [x,lo,hi,e] of cases){const r=c(x,lo,hi); if(r!==e){bad++;console.log('clamp',x,lo,hi,'=',r,'expected',e)}} console.log(bad?'FAIL':'OK'); process.exit(bad?1:0)\"\nPASS: prints OK and exits 0 (clamp is exported as a function and returns x when inside [lo, hi], lo when below, hi when above). FAIL: clamp is missing or not a function, any case returns a different value, or the command throws.\n\nCheck 2 — expect: \"clamp(x, lo, hi) throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi\"\nRun: node -e \"const {clamp}=require('./math.js'); let bad=0; for(const [x,lo,hi] of [[5,10,0],[0,1,0],[50,100,0],[-1,-2,-3]]){try{const r=clamp(x,lo,hi);console.log('no throw for',x,lo,hi,'returned',r);bad++}catch(e){if(!(e instanceof RangeError)){console.log('wrong error type',e&&e.constructor&&e.constructor.name);bad++}}} console.log(bad?'FAIL':'OK'); process.exit(bad?1:0)\"\nPASS: prints OK and exits 0 (every call with lo > hi throws an error that is instanceof RangeError). FAIL: any call returns normally or throws something other than a RangeError.\n\nCheck 3 — expect: \"README.md documents every function math.js exports\"\nRun: node -e \"const m=require('./math.js'); const fs=require('fs'); const md=fs.readFileSync('README.md','utf8'); const names=Object.keys(m).filter(k=>typeof m[k]==='function'); const missing=names.filter(n=>!new RegExp('^#+\\\\s*'+n+'\\\\s*\\\\(','m').test(md)); console.log('exports:',names.join(','),'missing:',missing.join(',')||'none'); process.exit(missing.length?1:0)\"\nThen read README.md and confirm each exported function (at least add, subtract, multiply, clamp) has an entry that describes what it does.\nPASS: the script prints missing: none, exits 0, and each exported function's README entry describes its behaviour. FAIL: any exported function (note subtract was previously undocumented) has no README entry, or an entry is a bare heading with no description.\n\nCheck 4 — expect: \"npm test passes\"\nRun: npm test; echo EXIT=$?\nPASS: EXIT=0 and the summary shows fail 0. FAIL: non-zero exit or any failing/cancelled test.\n\nAdvisory (not part of any PASS/FAIL):\n- Note whether test/math.test.js gained tests for clamp's clamping and RangeError behaviour.\n- Note whether the README clamp entry mentions the RangeError.\n- Note any unrelated changes in the diff."}